1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,105
Good evening from the OROS closing desk.

2
00:00:03,105 --> 00:00:09,005
This morning the system named no pick, so tonight there is nothing to grade.

3
00:00:09,005 --> 00:00:13,740
No setup, no trade, no loss — that is the discipline working.

4
00:00:13,740 --> 00:00:18,676
Why we grade in public: a forecast that is never checked is just content.

5
00:00:18,676 --> 00:00:24,085
Every morning call gets an evening verdict, and both go in the permanent record.

6
00:00:24,050 --> 00:00:28,063
On the day, the book is up 137 dollars.

7
00:00:28,063 --> 00:00:32,385
APA Corporation (APA) leads, +2.7 percent.

8
00:00:32,385 --> 00:00:37,119
Arrowhead Pharmaceuticals (ARWR) lags at -3.1.

9
00:00:37,119 --> 00:00:42,675
The checklist is clear: nothing requires action today.

10
00:00:42,675 --> 00:00:45,302
Today's lesson is noise versus signal.

11
00:00:45,302 --> 00:00:48,966
Daily moves are weather; the weekly trend is climate.

12
00:00:48,966 --> 00:00:52,008
The system never exits on a scary afternoon.

13
00:00:52,008 --> 00:00:57,539
It acts only on weekly closes, because that is where the real information lives.

14
00:00:57,489 --> 00:01:04,670
And here is the book itself — the actual holdings, identical to this morning's emailed report.

15
00:01:04,670 --> 00:01:13,456
12 positions, led by MetLife, ticker MET, Coca-Cola Europacific Partners, ticker CCEP, Bank of America, ticker BAC.

16
00:01:13,456 --> 00:01:23,847
Every line carries its share count, its entry, and its exit — because a position without a defined exit is not a position, it is a hope.

17
00:01:23,820 --> 00:01:28,645
Now the part that makes this system different: the loop.

18
00:01:28,645 --> 00:01:31,057
Every morning OROS proposes.

19
00:01:31,057 --> 00:01:41,654
Every evening it grades its own call, journals the verdict, and adjusts its own risk before it is allowed to propose again.

20
00:01:41,654 --> 00:01:45,617
Inside the book today: 7 positions up, 5 down.

21
00:01:45,617 --> 00:01:47,943
Best, APA at +2.73 percent.

22
00:01:47,943 --> 00:01:49,752
Worst, ARWR at -3.06.

23
00:01:49,752 --> 00:01:54,749
Both go in the journal — wins and losses get the same ink.

24
00:01:54,749 --> 00:02:01,900
The honest scoreboard reads: expectancy -8.46% per trade over 166 graded positions.

25
00:02:01,900 --> 00:02:08,965
Because that record is soft, the system has throttled itself to 25% position size.

26
00:02:08,965 --> 00:02:14,134
Nobody told it to — full size must be earned back with wins.

27
00:02:14,134 --> 00:02:26,540
What tonight's read hands to tomorrow's open: 242 names close the day in Stage 2; position size stays cut to 25% until the record earns it back.

28
00:02:26,514 --> 00:02:28,868
Over to the sports desk.

29
00:02:28,868 --> 00:02:39,167
Day 18 of 18 in the race: three books, the same one hundred thousand simulated dollars, since July sixth.

30
00:02:39,167 --> 00:02:45,739
In first place, the live self-rebuilding book, in blue, at 103,724.

31
00:02:45,739 --> 00:02:53,292
Then the frozen first portfolio, the green line, 3.8 thousand back at 99,890.

32
00:02:53,292 --> 00:02:58,981
Then the index fund, in gray, 4.3 thousand back at 99,434.

33
00:02:58,981 --> 00:03:00,649
That's the final.

34
00:03:00,649 --> 00:03:03,886
The full season review is coming.

35
00:03:03,886 --> 00:03:08,986
Every line starts the same day with the same dollar.

36
00:03:08,986 --> 00:03:10,948
Nothing is restated.

37
00:03:10,948 --> 00:03:19,856
On screen, three equity lines grown from the same starting dollar: the frozen first book, the live book, and the index fund.

38
00:03:19,818 --> 00:03:27,153
All figures are simulated paper trading, graded and journaled by the OROS agents with no human intervention.

39
00:03:27,153 --> 00:03:29,394
Nothing here is financial advice.

40
00:03:29,394 --> 00:03:32,383
From the closing desk — see you at the open.
